From: "Barak A. Pearlmutter" <barak+git@pearlmutter.net>
Date: Mon, 3 Oct 2022 19:38:46 +0100
Subject: i686 kernel version

On arch i686, there is code to fail for kernel version <2.2, but it's
written as a case statement that ends at version 4.x. The test is
altered to allow arbitrarily kernel versions >=2.2.
---
 config/_arch-n-opsys | 13 +++----------
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)
 mode change 100644 => 100755 config/_arch-n-opsys

diff --git a/config/_arch-n-opsys b/config/_arch-n-opsys
old mode 100644
new mode 100755
index 33cda40..bf7b4aa
--- a/config/_arch-n-opsys
+++ b/config/_arch-n-opsys
@@ -58,16 +58,9 @@ case `uname -s` in
       *86)
 	ARCH=x86
       # we no longer support Linux before the 2.2 kernel.
-	case `uname -r` in
-	  2.2.*) ;;
-	  2.3.*) ;;
-	  2.4.*) ;;
-	  2.5.*) ;;
-	  2.6.*) ;;
-	  3.*) ;;
-	  4.*) ;;
-	  *) exit 1 ;;
-	esac
+	if [ $(uname -r) '<' 2.2 ]; then
+	    exit 1
+	fi
 	;;
     # As long as we do not natively support the amd64 architecture,
     # we should fallback to the x86 compatibility mode.  --Stef
